\chapter{Introduction}\label{ch:overview}

This book retells the source code of a client application for Apple Vision Pro and iPhone that
shows immersive content rendered on another computer. The application is a fork of NVIDIA's
\emph{CloudXR Generic Viewer}~\cite{nvviewer}, the reference client for CloudXR on Apple
platforms. The fork keeps everything the original does and adds a second way of streaming:
Apple's \emph{Foveated Streaming} framework~\cite{applefov}, which visionOS provides as part of
the system, and its iOS counterpart, the \emph{StreamingSession} package~\cite{applesession}.

\section{Why foveation}

The human eye sees fine detail only in a small region around the point of fixation, the fovea,
which spans a couple of degrees of the visual field. Acuity falls quickly with eccentricity: a
few degrees away from the fixation point the eye already resolves several times less detail.
\emph{Foveated rendering} exploits this by spending effort where the user looks and saving it
in the periphery; Guenter et al.~\cite{guenter12} showed that rendering three nested layers at
decreasing resolution around a tracked gaze point is indistinguishable from full resolution
when the eye tracker is fast enough.

In remote rendering the scarce resource is not only GPU time but the network: a stereo stream at
the resolution of Vision Pro's displays needs many tens of megabits per second. With Foveated
Streaming the headset reports the \emph{approximate region} the user is looking at to the CloudXR
Runtime on the server, which uses it to distribute the video encoder's bit rate: the gaze region
is encoded at full quality and the periphery more coarsely. The foveation therefore happens in the
encoder, driven by the operating system, and not in this application. Two consequences shape the
code:
\begin{itemize}
\item The client never sees gaze data. visionOS sends the focus region to the CloudXR Runtime
  inside the session, and the runtime does not hand it to the streamed OpenXR application either
  (NVIDIA's documentation states this explicitly~\cite{nvfov}). Eye-tracking privacy is kept by
  construction.
\item The client does not draw the stream itself. The system presents it in an immersive space
  that the application declares with [[ImmersiveSpace(foveatedStreaming:)]], and the session
  opens and closes that space on its own.
\end{itemize}

\section{Two backends}

The configuration screen lets the user pick the \emph{streaming backend}:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Backend & Platform & Library \\
\midrule
CloudXR & visionOS, iOS & CloudXRKit Swift package (the original code path) \\
Foveated Streaming & visionOS & FoveatedStreaming system framework \\
Streaming Session & iOS & StreamingSession package, same API without eye tracking \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Having both in one application makes it possible to connect to the same server with and without
foveation and compare. It also keeps the fork close to NVIDIA's code, which matters because the
work is meant to be offered back to the original repository: the application keeps its name, its
targets and its hand-maintained Xcode project, and the changes are as small as the design allows.

The two libraries have similar but not identical APIs. Where CloudXRKit has a [[Session]] with a
[[state]], Foveated Streaming has a [[FoveatedStreamingSession]] with a [[status]]; CloudXRKit
disconnects synchronously, Foveated Streaming asynchronously; CloudXRKit's message channels
return a [[Bool]] when sending, Foveated Streaming's throw. Rather than sprinkle the views with
[[if backend == ...]] tests, the fork introduces a small protocol, [[ViewerSession]]
(Chapter~\ref{ch:abstraction}), and one adapter per library (Chapters~\ref{ch:classic}
and~\ref{ch:foveated}). The views talk to the protocol; only the places where the libraries
truly differ (how a connection is configured, how the stream is drawn) look at the backend.

\section{How to read this document}

This is a \emph{literate program} in the sense of Donald Knuth~\cite{knuth84}: a document written
for people, from which the program for the machine is extracted. The code appears in
\emph{chunks}. Each chunk has a name and a number, and is written
\[
  \langle\textit{name}\ \textsf{\small n}\rangle \equiv
\]
where it is defined. A chunk may use other chunks by name. When the same name is defined again,
the new text is appended to the old, and the header reads $+\!\equiv$. Below each chunk, a note
tells where it is continued and where it is used. The chunk index at the end of the book lists
every chunk.

A chunk named like a file is a \emph{root}: \emph{tangling} follows its references and writes the
file. The roots of this web are exactly the Swift sources of the application, its build
configuration, its entitlements and its two [[Info.plist]] files, so tangling rebuilds them from
this document. \emph{Weaving} produces the \LaTeX{} you are reading. The directory
[[LiterateP]] holds the web ([[chapters/*.nw]]), the tool [[lit.py]] that does both jobs using
Norman Ramsey's noweb notation~\cite{ramsey94}, and a [[Makefile]]. Running [[make check]]
tangles the web and compares every file, byte for byte, with the sources in the repository, so
this document cannot drift from the program without the check noticing. The Xcode project file
is left out: Xcode maintains it.

Many of NVIDIA's files end without a final newline. Their last chunk ends with the marker
[[@nonl]], which [[lit.py]] understands, and the note under the chunk says so.

\section{Plan of the book}

Chapter~\ref{ch:app} presents the application object, its scenes and the shared model.
Chapter~\ref{ch:abstraction} defines the backend-neutral vocabulary: backends, statuses, channels
and the [[ViewerSession]] protocol. Chapter~\ref{ch:classic} adapts CloudXRKit to it and shows
how a classic connection is configured; Chapter~\ref{ch:foveated} does the same for Foveated
Streaming and covers the build settings, entitlements and property lists it needs.
Chapter~\ref{ch:visionos} is the visionOS user interface and the window manager that coordinates
windows and immersive spaces; Chapter~\ref{ch:ios} the iOS one. Chapter~\ref{ch:messaging} is the
opaque data channel panel, and Chapter~\ref{ch:utilities} collects the remaining helpers.

